(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-pathophysiology-i-exam-2-immunity-quiz"]={"title":"Immunity Quiz 1 — Clinical Pathophysiology I","category":"Clinical Pathophysiology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What starts the antigen-dependent phase of B-cell maturation?","choices":["Exposure to the antigen","Expression of surface IgM","Release of perforin","Processing in the thymus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The antigen-dependent phase, in which the B cell differentiates into a plasma cell making specific antibody, is initiated by exposure to the antigen. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which surface marker identifies natural killer cells?","choices":["CD3","CD4","CD19","CD56"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Natural killer cells carry CD56, and many also carry CD16, which binds the Fc region of IgG. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 21, 34"},{"q":"Which form immediately precedes the segmented neutrophil?","choices":["Metamyelocyte","Myelocyte","Monoblast","Band form"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The neutrophil line runs myeloblast, promyelocyte, myelocyte, metamyelocyte, then the band form, then the segmented neutrophil. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin binds mast cells and basophils?","choices":["IgD","IgA","IgM","IgE"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. IgE binds mast cells and basophils through high-affinity receptors and contributes to allergy and parasite defense. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 44, 46"},{"q":"How do cytotoxic T cells kill infected cells?","choices":["Perforin and granzymes trigger apoptosis","Complement pores cause osmotic lysis","Antibody coating speeds their ingestion","Blocking the virus from attaching"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Perforin release is important to the effector function, and perforin with granzymes helps trigger target-cell apoptosis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 25, 31"},{"q":"How do antibodies bring about lysis of a target cell?","choices":["By recruiting the complement system","By releasing perforin themselves","By cross-linking soluble antigen","By coating the cell for phagocytes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Lysis, destruction of the cell, happens through incorporation with the complement system. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"Which complement fragment coats targets as an opsonin?","choices":["C5a","C3b","C3a","C9"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. C3b coats the target for opsonization by macrophages, while C3a, C4a and C5a promote the inflammatory response. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What does an adaptive B-cell or T-cell receptor recognize?","choices":["Patterns shared by groups of organisms","A particular epitope","Any foreign protein, whatever its shape","Antigens met in a previous exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adaptive specificity means B-cell and T-cell receptors recognize particular epitopes, the specific regions of an antigen. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"Which surface marker is found on T cells as a group?","choices":["CD3","CD4","CD8","CD19"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. T cells carry CD3, with CD4 or CD8 on the major subsets. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"How do changes in older adults affect immune responses?","choices":["Stronger responses to new antigens","Weaker responses to new antigens","Disappearance of autoantibodies","Return of maternal IgG protection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In older adults, changes in lymphocyte function and repertoire can reduce responses to new antigens, and autoantibodies become more common. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Lupus is an example of which hypersensitivity type?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lupus is a type III immune-complex example, alongside immune-complex glomerulonephritis or arthritis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What distinguishes primary from secondary immunodeficiency?","choices":["Primary is caused by medication","Primary follows a malignancy","Primary arises from poor nutrition","Primary is intrinsic, often genetic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Primary immunodeficiency is an intrinsic defect in immune development or function, often with a genetic basis; secondary immunodeficiency is reduced function from disease, medications, malignancy or inadequate nutrition. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 76, 77"},{"q":"Why is IgM especially effective at agglutination?","choices":["It crosses the placenta","It has many binding sites","It binds mast-cell receptors","It has a secretory component"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Secreted IgM is usually a pentamer that places many binding sites around one molecule, so it links antigen-bearing particles into clumps; agglutination is one of its main functions. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 45, 46, 47"},{"q":"Approximately what share of serum immunoglobulin is IgG?","choices":["Less than 1%","5–10%","10–15%","75–80%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. IgG is the most abundant class, approximately 75–80% of serum immunoglobulin (700–1700 mg/dL); reference intervals vary by laboratory. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"How does the secondary response differ from the primary response?","choices":["Faster and stronger, often IgG","Slower, and dominated by IgM","Same speed but with more IgE","It needs no memory cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Memory cells support a generally faster and stronger response on re-exposure, and class-switched antibodies, often IgG, may reach higher levels and persist longer. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 36, 37"},{"q":"Which of these is a T-cell-mediated hypersensitivity reaction?","choices":["Anaphylaxis to insect venom","ABO transfusion incompatibility","Serum sickness","Contact dermatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Contact dermatitis is type IV, mediated by sensitized CD4 or CD8 T cells, along with tuberculosis-related reactions and T-cell-mediated transplant rejection. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"What forms the antigen-binding site of an antibody?","choices":["The light-chain constant region alone","The heavy-chain constant region","Heavy and light variable regions together","The hinge region"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heavy- and light-chain variable regions jointly form the antigen-binding site at the tip of each arm. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 39, 41, 42"},{"q":"How does the membrane attack complex kill a target cell?","choices":["It triggers apoptosis with granzymes","It disrupts ionic and osmotic balance","It coats the cell for phagocytes","It blocks the cell's receptor binding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The pores damage the membrane and disrupt ionic and osmotic balance; ions and water move abnormally, and severe disruption leads to cell lysis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slides 68, 74"},{"q":"Helper T cells recognize antigenic peptides presented on which molecules?","choices":["Major histocompatibility complex class II","Major histocompatibility complex class I","Surface immunoglobulin on B cells","CD16 on natural killer cells"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Helper (CD4) T cells recognize antigenic peptides presented by major histocompatibility complex class II molecules. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How does neutralization protect without destroying the target?","choices":["It clumps the targets into lattices","It coats the targets for phagocytes","It blocks an essential host interaction","It assembles pores in their membranes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibody binding blocks an essential interaction with a host cell, preventing viral attachment or entry or a toxin reaching its target, and limits activity without physical destruction. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"What makes vaccination a form of ARTIFICIAL active immunity?","choices":["Antibodies come from another source","The antigen is met through ordinary contact","Antigen exposure is done on purpose","It acts with no prior sensitization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Active immunity is acquired by the body's own processes after exposure to the antigen; it is artificial when the exposure is deliberate, as with vaccination. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is precipitation, as an antibody function?","choices":["Clumping antigen-bearing cells","Coating a cell for ingestion","Cross-linking soluble antigens","Blocking a virus's attachment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies cross-link soluble antigens into larger complexes that can become insoluble and precipitate, which may help phagocytes clear them. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which leukocyte is the most numerous first responder, acting by phagocytosis?","choices":["Eosinophil","Basophil","Neutrophil","Monocyte"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils, the “poly” or PMN (polymorphonuclear leukocyte), are the most numerous first responders and act by phagocytosis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which organ produces many of the circulating complement components?","choices":["Bone marrow","Thymus","Spleen","Liver"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The liver produces many circulating complement components, and many circulate as inactive precursors until activation starts a regulated cascade. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"How does interstitial fluid enter a lymphatic capillary?","choices":["Between overlapping endothelial cells","Through smooth muscle pores in the wall","By active pumping across a basement membrane","Driven in by the heart's systolic pressure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The capillary wall is built of overlapping endothelial cells whose edges act as valves: they open to let interstitial fluid in and close behind it, so flow runs one way. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"What does a monocyte become once it enters tissue during inflammation?","choices":["A plasma cell","A macrophage","A mast cell","A neutrophil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Monocytes circulate in blood and enter tissues during inflammation, where tissue signals promote differentiation into macrophages. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"The Arthus reaction and serum sickness are examples of which hypersensitivity type?","choices":["Type I","Type II","Type III","Type IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Both are type III immune-complex reactions: the Arthus reaction is a local response in a previously sensitized person, and serum sickness is a systemic reaction to circulating immune complexes. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What determines an immunoglobulin's class?","choices":["Whether the light chain is kappa or lambda","The heavy-chain constant region","The variable-region sequence","The number of disulfide bonds"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heavy-chain constant region determines immunoglobulin class (heavy chain mu, delta, gamma, epsilon or alpha). — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which step follows adherence of the microbe to the phagocyte?","choices":["Pseudopods engulf the particle","Lysosome fuses with the vesicle","Residual material is exocytosed","Acid hydrolases digest the microbe"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sequence is: the microbe adheres; pseudopods engulf it into a phagosome; the phagosome fuses with a lysosome; enzymes digest it; residual material leaves by exocytosis. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which is an example of NATURAL passive immunity?","choices":["Rabies immune globulin given after exposure","Maternal IgG crossing the placenta","Antibodies made after a natural infection","A vaccine series that builds antibody"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Natural passive immunity is maternal IgG through the placenta and IgA in breast milk: preformed antibodies from another source, transferred naturally. — Immunity_Lecture_Recorded.pptx, Slide 12"}]};
